    "created_at": "2018-03-28T12:20:51Z",
    "updated_at": "2018-03-28T12:23:09Z",
    "body": "I don't think this use of strong is \"weird\". Whether something is deemed important is contextual (in this case, to the heading in question). It's never stated anywhere that every non-strong piece of text in the document, or every non-strong piece of text inside elements of the same local name, need to have the same level of importance. Indeed, that would be a very bad requirement to place on authors.\r\n\r\nIt's fine that in that one heading, the important part is emphasized by strong---that's its purpose. It remains fine to use strong in this way even if other headings in the document do not have parts of them that need particular emphasis.\r\n\r\nAs such, I don't think we'll be changing the example (or the definition of strong or small). But, perhaps there are some sentences in the above explanation that might be helpful to include in the spec, to help others who similarly misunderstand? Let us know.\r\n\r\nEdited to add: the level (\"weight\") of a heading is given by it's heading level, not by the tags inside of it. So in your example both headings are of the same weight.",
